.venv/
generated/
# ...except the planted red-baseline artifact. It is a two-file FIXTURE, not corpus data:
# it exists so tests/test_corpus_keeps_a_red_baseline.py has an input that compiles WRONG.
# The two real archives that used to carry that property were local-only and were deleted
# (FINDING-i-deleted-the-corpus.txt); a control that cannot survive a fresh checkout is a
# control that will go missing again.
!generated/plantedred-v4/
!generated/plantedred-v4/**
__pycache__/
*.pyc
examples/**/*.test
examples/tasks-api/tasks-api
bin/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# local run artifacts (distillation farm + raw traces)
farm/
traces/
# logs/* — NOT logs/. Git cannot re-include a file whose parent DIRECTORY is
# excluded, so with `logs/` the negation below is dead text that silently does
# nothing. Excluding the CONTENTS instead leaves the directory visible, which is
# what makes the exception work. (Found the way everything else was found today:
# the commit refused the file and I read the error instead of retrying it.)
# ⚠️ INVERTED 31 July, after this list went stale for the SEVENTH time. Every previous
# note below explains the mechanism as though naming it were the fix; six of them add one
# more prefix. It kept happening because the rule enumerated what to KEEP, so anything
# with a new name was silently dropped, and the drop is invisible — `git commit` says
# "nothing to commit" and looks like success.
#
# Measured before changing it: of 875 files in logs/, 852 are .log/.out/.nohup/.summary —
# raw run output, regenerable by definition. The other 23 are records. Sixteen of those
# were sitting UNTRACKED under the old rule: twelve AUDIT-*-BEFORE.txt baselines and four
# repair-survival*.tsv gate baselines, which cannot be regenerated at all because the gate
# they measured no longer exists in the code.
#
# So the rule now enumerates what to DISCARD, and a record with a name nobody predicted is
# kept by default. That is the direction the last six notes each argued for and none took.
logs/*.log
logs/*.out
logs/*.nohup
logs/*.summary
logs/.current-*

# ...except the predictions. These are not run artifacts, they are the method:
# each one is a claim written down BEFORE its measurement, including the outcome
# that would refute it. That ordering is the only thing separating "I predicted
# this" from "I explained this afterwards", and it is worth exactly as much as it
# is verifiable. Today it nearly wasn't: I stamped these files with times I never
# read off a clock, drifting an hour into the future, and the ordering was only
# recoverable because GIT held the real times. The timekeeper should hold the
# record too. Raw logs stay ignored — they are regenerable; a prediction is not.
# ...and the corpus verdict baseline. This one looks like a raw log and is not: the
# sweep can be re-run, but it cannot be re-run AS OF YESTERDAY, and comparing today's
# verdicts against a previous day is the entire purpose. Untracked, it would have been
# regenerable in exactly the sense that lost me a row list this morning and cost a
# published claim I then had to withdraw. 150 lines.
#
# Caught because the commit that added the file said "tracked in git on purpose" and the
# file was silently ignored — the claim and the behaviour disagreed, and only running
# `git ls-files` on it said so.
# ...and the pending list. Same class as the predictions and for the same reason: it is a
# set of claims with the outcome that would refute each one, written BEFORE the measurement.
# Untracked it is a scratch note; tracked it is checkable against what actually happened.
# Caught the way the rest of this file was: the commit said "nothing to commit" and I read
# that instead of retrying it.
# ...AND EVERY OTHER GENERATION AND BASELINE OF IT, which the single-file rule above did not
# cover and nothing announced. Found 29 July by creating logs/hole-hunt-rows-v5.tsv and
# asking git: it is ignored. That file is the ENTIRE OUTPUT of the capstone redraw — the
# comparison this whole campaign exists to make — and it would have been written, read once,
# and lost, with the tracked v4 baseline sitting beside it looking like the pair was safe.
#
# The same hole swallowed three baselines already on disk and untracked:
#   hole-hunt-rows-before-walk-fix.tsv    <- the BEFORE half of _resweep_report's comparison
#   hole-hunt-rows-before-redraw.tsv
#   hole-hunt-rows-before-noapply-fix.tsv
#
# ...and reachability's rows, whose own code PRINTS "(tracked — a changed verdict shows as a
# diff)" while git ignored the file. A tool asserting its output is tracked is not evidence
# that it is; `git ls-files` is. That line has been printing a false claim since the tool
# was written.
# Do not verify this with `git check-ignore` — it reports pattern matching and says
# nothing about tracking, so it answered "ignored" for a file that was already
# committed and diffing correctly. The question is whether a CHANGE shows up:
#   git ls-files --error-unmatch logs/hole-hunt-rows.tsv   (tracked?)
#   append a row, then `git status --short`                (does it diff?)

# ...and the findings. A FINDING is a prediction's sibling: a measured, model-free
# result (a mutation that survives, a BEFORE artifact that proves a hole ships) —
# not regenerable, because it records what a specific artifact did at a specific
# commit. Same reason as the predictions; same exception.
# ...and the RESULTS, which are what a PREDICTION becomes once the run lands. Keeping the
# prediction and ignoring the result is the worst of the three options: it preserves the
# guess and discards the grade, so a later reader finds five confident predictions and no
# record of which ones missed. Caught the first time a RESULT was written — the commit
# reported "nothing to commit, working tree clean" for a file that had just been created,
# which is exactly the silent-ignore failure the hole-hunt-rows note above describes.
# ...and the NOTES, the fourth member of the family. A NOTE records a CONDITION the
# measurement was taken under — "these two of twenty-two specs were drawn against a second
# server process after a reboot" — which is regenerable from nothing, because the condition
# is gone the moment the run ends. It is the caveat that has to travel attached to a number
# or it is not a caveat, it is a thing someone finds later. Same silent-ignore catch as the
# four above, on the fourth consecutive occasion: `git add` refused the path and printed the
# hint, which is louder than the "nothing to commit" that caught the others and is the only
# reason this rule exists before the number it qualifies was read rather than after.
# ...and the PREREGS, which are the same thing as a PREDICTION under a different filename — and
# that is the whole failure. This list has now gone stale SIX times, and every previous note
# above explains the mechanism as though naming it were the fix. It is not. Five of the six were
# a new KIND of record; this one is a new NAME for a record already covered in spirit, which is
# worse, because nothing about writing "PREREG" instead of "PREDICTION" feels like introducing an
# untracked file class. Both PREREG files on disk were untracked when this was found:
#   PREREG-cross-spec-name-swap-ratelimit.txt   <- backs a row in the CLOSED ten-arm distribution
#   PREREG-cross-server-control-replication.txt
# 77 of 77 PREDICTION-*.txt were tracked. 0 of 2 PREREG-*.txt were.
#
# An untracked pre-registration is not a weaker pre-registration, it is NOT ONE: its entire
# epistemic value is that git can show the prediction predates the result, and that is exactly
# the property the .gitignore comment at line 28 says these files exist to have. The ratelimit
# arm's prediction has been uncheckable since it was written.
#
# Caught by "nothing to commit, working tree clean" — the sixth consecutive time, and the one
# failure mode line 48 explicitly warns about reading past. Do not add a seventh note. If a new
# record filename appears, run `git ls-files --error-unmatch` on it BEFORE it holds a claim.

# The !logs/... exceptions that lived here are gone: with the rule inverted they were dead
# text, and this file already warns that dead text silently does nothing. 9 removed.
